% ============================================================================
% rainforest-layer — each of the four rainforest layers and a one-fact
% description of it (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A beginner ecology fact: a rainforest is not one flat habitat — it is FOUR
% stacked layers, from the treetops down to the ground, and each layer has its
% own defining character. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? rainforest_layer(canopy, $D)   % deep_treetop_vegetation_layer
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `rainforest_layer(layer, description)` carrying the table's
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the description atom WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on a word that is not one of the four layers.
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind the description and recall the layer:
%
%     ? rainforest_layer($L, tallest_trees_dominate_skyline)   % emergent
%
% The four layers, top to bottom, as a little truth table:
%
%     layer          description (this table's label)         the cited quote
%     ------------   ----------------------------------------   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     emergent       tallest_trees_dominate_skyline              "The top layer of the rainforest is the emergent layer. Here, trees as tall as
%                                                                  60 meters (200 feet) dominate the skyline."
%     canopy         deep_treetop_vegetation_layer                "Beneath the emergent layer is the canopy, a deep layer of vegetation roughly
%                                                                  six meters (20 feet) thick."
%     understory     dark_humid_layer_below_canopy                "Located several meters below the canopy, the
%                                                                  understory is an even darker, stiller and more humid
%                                                                  environment."
%     forest_floor   darkest_layer_hard_for_plants_to_grow        "The forest floor is the darkest of all rainforest layers, making it extremely
%                                                                  difficult for plants to grow."
%
% Provenance: quoted from National Geographic Education's "Rain Forest"
% encyclopedic entry. Each row now carries ITS OWN sentence (RS-5e per-row
% provenance, #14986); the envelope holds the FRAMING span. Until then the
% envelope was the EMERGENT row's sentence, so a recall of the forest floor
% was warranted by a sentence about the treetops.
%
% RE-MEASURED 2026-09-13 UTC with a RAW fetch, not a summarizing fetcher.
% (This paragraph used to say "quoted verbatim ... WebFetch-verified before
% writing". A summarizing fetcher is not an instrument for a verbatim span —
% see biology/kingdoms.adj, where a WebFetch-"verified" line turned out to
% contain a phrase the page does not have.) Each of the four row spans and
% the framing span occurs EXACTLY ONCE in the page's rendered text (inline
% tags removed with no separator, block tags as breaks). Controls: a nonsense
% span scores zero, and a near-miss of each of the five — its final word
% altered — scores zero.
%
% TWO ROWS ARE READ, TWO REST ON A READING, and the tier says which:
%
%   forest_floor  READ. "the darkest of all rainforest layers" is on the page,
%                 once. The bounding-language audit flagged this row as a
%                 superlative with no bounding language in its provenance —
%                 the claim was never the problem, only that the evidence sat
%                 outside the machine-readable envelope.
%   understory    READ. darker, more humid, below the canopy: all stated.
%   emergent      READING. "tallest" occurs ZERO times on the page. It says
%                 "The top layer of the rainforest is the emergent layer" and
%                 "trees as tall as 60 meters". `tallest` restates that.
%   canopy        READING. "treetop" occurs ZERO times on the page. It says
%                 "a deep layer of vegetation" whose leaves form a "roof"
%                 over the layers below. `treetop` restates that.
%
% The two readings carry `trust inferred`. NOTE THE COST, because it is a
% real limitation rather than a clean win: the five tiers are one dimension,
% so marking those rows `inferred` DROPS the `consensus` signal that says
% this is a teaching resource rather than a primary source. Both facts are
% true of those rows and only one fits. Not resolved here.
%
% `trust consensus` on the envelope — National Geographic Education is a
% reputable education organization, not a primary government source, the same
% tier this stdlib already reserves for its other non-.gov sources (e.g. Wikipedia's
% Greek-alphabet entry, Speakspeak's plural-ending article). A word that is
% NOT one of these four layers — "soil layer", "topsoil", or a made-up layer
% name — is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on it rather than
% inventing a description. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table rainforest_layer {
    columns layer, description

    row (emergent, tallest_trees_dominate_skyline) {
        % READING, not a quotation. The page says "The top layer of
        % the rainforest is the emergent layer" and "trees as tall as
        % 60 meters"; the word "tallest" occurs ZERO times on it.
        % Position at the top plus that height is where `tallest`
        % comes from, so this row carries `trust inferred`.
        source "The top layer of the rainforest is the emergent layer. Here, trees as tall as 60 meters (200 feet) dominate the skyline."
        trust inferred
    }
    row (canopy, deep_treetop_vegetation_layer) {
        % READING, not a quotation. "treetop" occurs ZERO times on the
        % page. It says the canopy is "a deep layer of vegetation" and
        % that its leaves and branches form a "roof" over the layers
        % below -- which is what `treetop` restates.
        source "Beneath the emergent layer is the canopy, a deep layer of vegetation roughly six meters (20 feet) thick."
        trust inferred
    }
    row (understory, dark_humid_layer_below_canopy) {
        source "Located several meters below the canopy, the understory is an even darker, stiller and more humid environment."
    }
    row (forest_floor, darkest_layer_hard_for_plants_to_grow) {
        % The superlative IS stated: "the darkest of all rainforest
        % layers" occurs once. This row was flagged by the bounding-
        % language audit only because the evidence sat outside the
        % machine-readable envelope, which is what RS-5e fixes.
        source "The forest floor is the darkest of all rainforest layers, making it extremely difficult for plants to grow."
    }

    % The envelope is the FRAMING span -- what defends the table as a
    % whole: that rainforests have these four layers, and which they are.
    % Until #14986 it was the EMERGENT row's own sentence, so a recall of
    % the forest floor was warranted by a sentence about the treetops.
    source "Most rainforests are structured in four layers: emergent, canopy, understory and forest floor."
    locator "https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/rain-forest/"
    trust consensus
}
